            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The west symbolizes endings, completion, decline, or death (associated with the setting sun), the sea (from the Hebrew 'yam'), infinite separation (when paired with east), and approaching God's presence (based on tabernacle orientation).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The west symbolizes where a course ends, what is behind, or the destination. This is rooted in the biblical compass facing east, which places the western sea ('yam') at the back, connecting it to the 'after/back/end' word-family, and marking complete separation when paired with east.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Endings/death (via sunset), the Mediterranean Sea (yam), separation (merism), and tabernacle approach.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Behind/last/destination (via eastward spatial orientation), the sea (yam), and complete separation.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book reclassifies the concept of 'endings' from a solar metaphor (sunset representing decline and death) to a spatial-linguistic metaphor (west as 'behind' or 'last' because the biblical compass faces east), while discarding the tabernacle-approach symbolism as primary.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus predominantly grounds the symbolism of endings in the setting sun (implying death/decline), whereas the book grounds it in the Hebrew orientational compass (where west means 'back' or 'after'), yielding a focus on destination and posteriority.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both agree on the west's association with the sea ('yam'), the concept of endings or completion, and its use in an east-west merism for total separation. The book refines the concept of 'endings' by anchoring it in Hebrew directional vocabulary (front=east, back=west) rather than the solar cycle, shifting the symbolic nuance.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's focus on spatial orientation may artificially exclude valid biblical solar imagery (e.g., Psalm 50:1, 'from the rising of the sun to its setting') that historically informed the Christian symbolic association of the west with sunset, decline, and evening.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
